@section The Metacircular Evaluator

Our evaluator for the object language will be implemented as a Rust
program.  It may seem circular to think about evaluating a language with an
evaluator that is itself a program.  However, evaluation is a process, so it
is appropriate to describe the evaluation process as a program, which, after
all, is our tool for describing processes.@footnote{Even so, there will remain
important aspects of the evaluation process that are not elucidated by our
evaluator.  The most important of these are the detailed mechanisms by which
procedures call other procedures and return values to their callers.  We will
address these issues in @ref{Chapter 5}, where we take a closer look at the
evaluation process by implementing the evaluator as a simple register machine.}
An evaluator that is written in the same language that it evaluates is said to
be @newterm{metacircular}.

The object language of this chapter is the edition's documented Rust subset.
Every object program is ordinary Rust source in the pinned edition.  Before
any effect can occur, the complete source is admitted by
@code{sicp_runtime::host::admit}, which lexes, parses, and checks it and
answers a typed @code{CheckedProgram}; a rejection is a typed @code{Diag}
(@code{Syntax}, @code{Type}, @code{Ownership}, or @code{Unsupported}) and no
evaluator is entered.  The same checked representation is executed by every
teaching engine of this book: the direct evaluator and analyzer of this
section, the explicit-control evaluator of @ref{5.4}, and the compiler of
@ref{5.5}.  A dynamic value inside an engine can therefore never let a source
program around Rust's static checks.

The metacircular evaluator is essentially a formulation of the environment
model of evaluation described in @ref{3.2}, written in the language it
evaluates.  Recall that the model has two basic parts:

@enumerate 1

@item
To evaluate a call (a compound expression other than a special form), evaluate
the subexpressions from left to right and then apply the value of the operator
subexpression to the values of the operand subexpressions.

@item
To apply a function or closure to a set of arguments, evaluate the body of the
procedure in a new environment.  To construct this environment, extend the
environment part of the procedure object by a frame in which the formal
parameters of the procedure are bound to the arguments to which the procedure
is applied.

@end enumerate

@noindent
These two rules describe the essence of the evaluation process, a basic cycle
in which expressions to be evaluated in environments are reduced to procedures
to be applied to arguments, which in turn are reduced to new expressions to be
evaluated in new environments, and so on, until we get down to names, whose
values are looked up in the environment, and to the primitive steps of the
object language, which are applied directly (see @ref{Figure 4.1}).@footnote{If
we grant ourselves the ability to apply primitives, then what remains for us to
implement in the evaluator?  The job of the evaluator is not to specify the
primitives of the language, but rather to provide the connective tissue---the
means of combination and the means of abstraction---that binds a collection of
primitives to form a language.  Specifically:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The evaluator enables us to deal with nested expressions.  For example,
although simply applying primitives would suffice for evaluating the expression
@code{1 + 6}, it is not adequate for handling @code{1 + 2 * 3}.  As far as the
primitive operation of addition is concerned, its operands must be numbers,
and it would choke if we passed it the expression @code{2 * 3} as an operand.
One important role of the evaluator is to choreograph composition so that
@code{2 * 3} is reduced to 6 before being used as an operand of @code{+}.

@item
The evaluator allows us to use variables.  For example, the primitive
operation for addition has no way to deal with expressions such as
@code{x + 1}.  We need an evaluator to keep track of variables and obtain
their values before invoking the primitive operations.

@item
The evaluator allows us to define compound procedures.  This involves keeping
track of procedure definitions, knowing how to use these definitions in
evaluating expressions, and providing a mechanism that enables procedures to
accept arguments.

@item
The evaluator provides the special forms, which must be evaluated differently
from procedure calls.

@end itemize
}
This evaluation cycle will be embodied by the interaction between the two
critical functions in the evaluator, @code{evaluate} and @code{apply}, which
are described in @ref{4.1.1} (see @ref{Figure 4.1}).

