The implementation of the evaluator will depend upon functions that define the
@newterm{syntax} of the expressions to be evaluated.  We will use data
abstraction to make the evaluator independent of the representation of the
language.  In this edition the expressions of the object language are typed
syntax data: closed Rust @code{enum} and @code{struct} values, not text and not
one universal dynamic form.  The checked front end builds the same kind of
tree from Rust source---each node of a @code{CheckedProgram} classifies itself
by variant---and the metacircular evaluator of this chapter classifies its own
@code{Expr} data with the predicates and selectors of @ref{4.1.2}, so the
evaluator is never coupled to a surface spelling.  There are also operations,
described in @ref{4.1.3}, that specify the representation of procedures and
environments.  For example, @code{Value::Closure} packages a procedure's
parameters and body together with the environment it captures as an index into
an owned arena of frames, @code{lookup} accesses the values of variables, and
@code{apply} applies a function or closure to a given argument vector.

The restrictions of the object language are part of its semantics, not
accidents of our implementation.  Values are owned unless their type is a
reference: passing a non-@code{Copy} value by value moves it, and an explicit
clone is a separate operation.  Closures capture by Rust's rules---by
reference, by mutable reference, or by @code{move}---and escape only in the
admitted boxed-closure forms.  The guest admits no @code{Rc}, @code{Arc},
@code{RefCell}, raw pointers, or @code{unsafe}.  Integer computation is
@code{i64}, with @code{usize} reserved for lengths, indexes, and arena or
machine identifiers; overflow and division by zero trap, division truncates
toward zero, and there is no implicit numeric conversion.  Conditions are
@code{bool}; there is no truthiness.  Our evaluator must reproduce these rules
for its object programs rather than substitute the host's habits.

