@subsection The Core of the Evaluator

The evaluation process can be described as the interaction between two
functions: @code{evaluate} and @code{apply}.

@subsubheading Eval

@code{Evaluate} takes as arguments an expression and an environment.  It
classifies the expression and directs its evaluation.  @code{Evaluate} is
structured as a case analysis of the syntactic type of the expression to be
evaluated.  To keep the procedure general, we express the determination of the
type of an expression abstractly, making no commitment to any particular
representation for the various types of expressions.  Each type of expression
has a predicate that tests for it and an abstract means for selecting its
parts.  This @newterm{abstract syntax} makes it easy to see how we can change
the syntax of the language by using the same evaluator, but with a different
collection of syntax procedures.

@noindent
@b{Primitive expressions}

@itemize @bullet

@item
For self-evaluating expressions, such as integers, Booleans, the unit value,
and strings, @code{evaluate} returns the expression's value directly.

@item
@code{Evaluate} must look up variables in the environment to find their
values.  A local name resolves in the current frame chain; a top-level
function name resolves in the global frame.

@end itemize

@noindent
@b{Special forms}

@itemize @bullet

@item
A binding (@code{let} or @code{let mut}) evaluates its initializer and adds a
new binding to a frame.  Bindings in a block are sequential, and a later
binding may shadow an earlier one; assignment mutates an existing place and
never creates a binding.

@item
An @code{if} expression requires special processing of its parts, so as to
evaluate the consequent if the predicate is true, and otherwise to evaluate
the alternative.  The predicate must be a @code{bool}.  The related
@code{if let} and @code{match} forms bind names from a pattern and likewise
choose one branch to evaluate.

@item
A closure expression must be transformed into an applicable procedure by
packaging together the parameters and body it specifies with the environment
of the evaluation.

@item
A block requires evaluating its sequence of statements in the order in which
they appear and then its tail expression, whose value is the value of the
block.  The control flows @code{return}, @code{break}, and @code{continue} are
values of the evaluator's flow type, never the host's control flow.

@item
The loop forms @code{loop}, @code{while}, @code{while let}, and @code{for}
evaluate their bodies repeatedly under their own exit rules.

@item
The Boolean operators @code{&&} and @code{||} are special forms as well: they
stop at the first operand that decides the result.

@end itemize

@noindent
@b{Combinations}

@itemize @bullet

@item
For a procedure application, @code{evaluate} must recursively evaluate the
operator part and the operands of the call, from left to right.  The resulting
procedure and arguments are passed to @code{apply}, which handles the actual
procedure application.

@end itemize

@noindent
The category of quoted expressions has no counterpart in this object language:
the guest has no quotation and no reader.  Its lesson---that syntax is
data---survives in the other direction.  The evaluator consumes typed data
(@code{variable("x")} constructs a datum; it does not spell one), and the
query and machine lessons of @ref{4.4} and @ref{5.1} build their programs
from typed constructors as well.

@noindent
Here is the dispatch of @code{evaluate} in the metacircular evaluator, which
is ordinary Rust source admitted like any other guest program:

@example rust
fn evaluate(
    expression: &Expr,
    environment: usize,
    global: usize,
    program: &Program,
    store: &mut Store,
) -> Result<Value, Fault> @{
    match expression @{
        Expr::Integer(value) => Ok(Value::Integer(*value)),
        Expr::Boolean(value) => Ok(Value::Boolean(*value)),
        Expr::Variable(name) => @{
            match lookup(store, Some(environment), name) @{
                Some(value) => Ok(value),
                None => Err(Fault::Unbound),
            @}
        @},
        Expr::Add(left, right) => @{
            let first =
                evaluate(left, environment, global, program, store)?;
            let second =
                evaluate(right, environment, global, program, store)?;
            match (first, second) @{
                (Value::Integer(a), Value::Integer(b)) => @{
                    Ok(Value::Integer(a + b))
                @}
                _ => Err(Fault::Type),
            @}
        @},
        Expr::If @{ test, yes, no @} => @{
            let cond =
                evaluate(test, environment, global, program, store)?;
            match cond @{
                Value::Boolean(true) => @{
                    evaluate(yes, environment, global, program, store)
                @}
                Value::Boolean(false) => @{
                    evaluate(no, environment, global, program, store)
                @}
                _ => Err(Fault::Type),
            @}
        @},
        Expr::Let @{ name, value, body @} => @{
            let initial =
                evaluate(value, environment, global, program, store)?;
            let child = new_frame(store, Some(environment));
            store.frames[child].bindings.insert(name.clone(), initial);
            evaluate(body, child, global, program, store)
        @},
        Expr::Lambda @{ parameters, body @} => Ok(Value::Closure @{
            parameters: parameters.clone(),
            body: body.clone(),
            environment,
        @}),
        Expr::Call @{ function, arguments @} => @{
            let callable = evaluate(
                function,
                environment,
                global,
                program,
                store,
            )?;
            let mut values = Vec::new();
            for argument in arguments.iter() @{
                let value = evaluate(
                    argument,
                    environment,
                    global,
                    program,
                    store,
                )?;
                values.push(value);
            @}
            apply(callable, values, global, program, store)
        @},
        @r{…}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The remaining arms evaluate @code{Subtract}, @code{Multiply}, and
@code{Equal} in the same left-to-right way as @code{Add}; every operand is a
@code{Value} the recursive call produced, and every type error is the typed
fault @code{Fault::Type}.  For clarity, @code{evaluate} is written here as one
@code{match} per syntactic type.  The completed checked evaluator classifies
its larger surface the same way, and in most language implementations
dispatching on the type of an expression is done in a data-directed style.
This allows a user to add new types of expressions that the evaluator can
distinguish, without modifying the definition of @code{evaluate} itself.
(See @ref{Exercise 4.3}.)

@subsubheading Apply

@code{Apply} takes two arguments, a procedure and a vector of arguments to
which the procedure should be applied.  @code{Apply} classifies procedures
into two kinds: it applies a top-level function by consulting the program's
function table, and it applies a closure by sequentially evaluating the
expression that makes up the body of the procedure.  The environment for the
evaluation of the body of a closure is constructed by extending the
environment the closure captured with a frame that binds the parameters of the
procedure to the arguments to which the procedure is to be applied.  Here is
the application half of @code{apply}:

@example rust
fn apply(
    callable: Value,
    arguments: Vec<Value>,
    global: usize,
    program: &Program,
    store: &mut Store,
) -> Result<Value, Fault> @{
    match callable @{
        Value::Function(index) => @{
            let definition = match program.functions.get(index) @{
                Some(definition) => definition,
                None => return Err(Fault::Unbound),
            @};
            if definition.parameters.len() != arguments.len() @{
                return Err(Fault::Arity);
            @}
            let child = new_frame(store, Some(global));
            let pending = arguments.into_iter();
            for (name, value) in
                definition.parameters.iter().zip(pending)
            @{
                store.frames[child]
                    .bindings
                    .insert(name.clone(), value);
            @}
            evaluate(&definition.body, child, global, program, store)
        @},
        Value::Closure @{ parameters, body, environment @} => @{
            if parameters.len() != arguments.len() @{
                return Err(Fault::Arity);
            @}
            let child = new_frame(store, Some(environment));
            let pending = arguments.into_iter();
            for (name, value) in
                parameters.into_iter().zip(pending)
            @{
                store.frames[child].bindings.insert(name, value);
            @}
            evaluate(&body, child, global, program, store)
        @},
        _ => Err(Fault::NotCallable),
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
A closure's environment is an index into the owned vector arena
@code{Store}---@code{frames: Vec<Frame>}---@code{usize} has Rust's
@code{Copy} behavior, so capturing the environment copies the index and
nothing else.  Recursive calls, escaping closures, and shared bindings
therefore need no reference counting and grant no alias that Rust's ownership
rules would forbid.

@subsubheading Procedure arguments

When @code{evaluate} processes a procedure application, it evaluates the
operands of the call and collects the corresponding values:@footnote{We could
have simplified the call clause in @code{evaluate} by collecting the evaluated
operands with an iterator adapter rather than writing an explicit loop.  We
chose not to hide the loop here to emphasize the fact that the evaluator can
be implemented without any use of higher-order procedures (and thus could be
written in a language that doesn't have higher-order procedures), even though
the language that it supports will include higher-order procedures.}

@example rust
let mut values = Vec::new();
for argument in arguments.iter() @{
    let value =
        evaluate(argument, environment, global, program, store)?;
    values.push(value);
@}
apply(callable, values, global, program, store)
@end example

@noindent
This loop fixes the object language's operand order by construction: the
operands of a call are evaluated from left to right.  The checked language
makes the same promise for nested operands and method receivers, so the
evaluator's order is observable in the effects of its object programs (see
@ref{Exercise 4.1}).

@subsubheading Conditionals

@code{evaluate} evaluates the predicate part of an @code{If} expression in the
given environment.  If the result is true, @code{evaluate} evaluates the
consequent, otherwise it evaluates the alternative:

@example rust
Expr::If @{ test, yes, no @} => @{
    let cond =
        evaluate(test, environment, global, program, store)?;
    match cond @{
        Value::Boolean(true) => @{
            evaluate(yes, environment, global, program, store)
        @}
        Value::Boolean(false) => @{
            evaluate(no, environment, global, program, store)
        @}
        _ => Err(Fault::Type),
    @}
@},
@end example

@noindent
The use of the matched @code{Value::Boolean} here highlights the issue of the
connection between an implemented language and an implementation language.
The predicate is evaluated in the language being implemented and thus yields a
value in that language; the interpreter translates that value into the value
that can be tested by the @code{if} and @code{match} of Rust: the metacircular
representation of truth might not be the same as that of the host.@footnote{Here
the language being implemented is the Rust guest subset and the
implementation language is Rust.  Both demand an explicit @code{bool}, so
@code{Fault::Type} is raised for anything else; an object language with
truthiness would reintroduce the translation this paragraph describes.
Contemplation of the meaning of that translation yields expansion of
consciousness without the abuse of substance.}

@subsubheading Sequences

The block evaluator is used by @code{apply} to evaluate the body of a
procedure and by @code{evaluate} to evaluate any block.  It takes as
arguments a sequence of statements and a tail expression and an environment,
and evaluates the statements in the order in which they occur.  The value
returned is the value of the final expression.  The completed checked
evaluator threads explicit flows through the same loop:

@example rust
fn eval_block(
    &mut self,
    block: &HirBlock,
    frame: usize,
) -> Result<Flow, TrapReport> @{
    for stmt in &block.stmts @{
        let flow = @r{…};
        if !matches!(flow, Flow::Value(_)) @{
            return Ok(flow);
        @}
    @}
    match &block.tail @{
        Some(tail) => self.eval_expr(tail, frame),
        None => Ok(Flow::Value(HostValue::Unit)),
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{Flow} carries @code{Value}, @code{Return}, @code{Break}, and
@code{Continue} through the evaluator as data, so a @code{return} inside a
nested expression reaches its function without any use of host
nonlocal control.@footnote{These selectors for a sequence of statements---and
the corresponding ones for a list of operands---are not intended as a data
abstraction.  They are introduced as mnemonic names for the basic vector
operations to make it easier to understand the explicit-control evaluator in
@ref{5.4}.}

@subsubheading Assignments and definitions

The following clause handles a binding.  It evaluates the initializer and
transmits the name and the resulting value to the frame to be installed in the
designated environment.  Bindings are definitions; assignment is the separate
act of changing a mutable place.

@example rust
Expr::Let @{ name, value, body @} => @{
    let initial = evaluate(value, environment, global, program, store)?;
    let child = new_frame(store, Some(environment));
    store.frames[child].bindings.insert(name.clone(), initial);
    evaluate(body, child, global, program, store)
@},
@end example

@noindent
Assignment in the checked language writes an existing binding through the
store's mutable access and yields the unit value.@footnote{As we said when we
introduced @code{let} and assignment, the value of an assignment is fixed by
the object language: it is the unit value in Rust, so the evaluator returns
@code{HostValue::Unit} there rather than a status symbol.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.1}Exercise 4.1:} Notice that we cannot tell from a
call alone which way the metacircular evaluator evaluates its operands.  In
this edition the order is not inherited from the host: Rust evaluates a
procedure's arguments from left to right by its own fixed rule, but the
object-language order is fixed by the @code{list_of_values} recursion this
book writes out, and the exercise is to pin that order down by construction
for the object language, where the host's rule does not reach.  Write a version of
@code{list_of_values} that evaluates operands from left to right by
construction.  Also write a version of @code{list_of_values} that evaluates
operands from right to left.
@end quotation
