@subsection Representing Expressions

The evaluator is reminiscent of the symbolic differentiation program discussed
in @ref{2.3.2}.  Both programs operate on symbolic expressions.  In
both programs, the result of operating on a compound expression is determined
by operating recursively on the pieces of the expression and combining the
results in a way that depends on the type of the expression.  In both programs
we used data abstraction to decouple the general rules of operation from the
details of how expressions are represented.  In the differentiation program
this meant that the same differentiation procedure could deal with algebraic
expressions in prefix form, in infix form, or in some other form.  For the
evaluator, this means that the syntax of the language being evaluated is
determined solely by the procedures that classify expressions and extract
their pieces.

Here is the specification of the syntax of our language.  Each item names the
Rust constructor that builds the form; in the text we abbreviate constructor
expressions in a compact notation, for example @code{Call(f, args)} for a
call node with operator @code{f} and operand vector @code{args}.  This is
notation for typed data---each abbreviation expands to the constructor call it
names---and is not a surface language.

@itemize

@item
The only self-evaluating items are integers and Booleans; the completed
checked language additionally has the unit value and strings:

@example rust
fn boxed(expression: Expr) -> Box<Expr> @{ Box::new(expression) @}
fn integer(value: i64) -> Expr @{ Expr::Integer(value) @}
fn one(expression: Expr) -> Vec<Expr> @{ vec![expression] @}
@end example

@item
Variables are represented by names carried in the data:

@example rust
fn variable(name: &str) -> Expr @{
    Expr::Variable(String::from(name))
@}
@end example

@item
Data are constructed, never read back from text.  A datum of the object
language is an ordinary Rust value; the expression that denotes the symbol
@code{x} is built by @code{variable("x")}, and the completed checked language
builds structured data with @code{vec!}, @code{Box::new}, structs, and enums
of the admitted grammar.

@item
Bindings have the form @code{Let(name, value, body)}:

@example rust
Expr::Let @{
    name: String::from("offset"),
    value: boxed(integer(3)),
    body: boxed(@r{…}),
@}
@end example

@noindent
and the completed checked language also has the block statement
@code{let name [: T] = e;}, its mutable form @code{let mut}, and assignment
to a mutable place.  Binding is sequential within a block and a later binding
shadows an earlier one.

@item
Closures are lists of parameter names together with a body, packaged with
their environment when evaluated:

@example rust
Expr::Lambda @{ parameters: Vec<String>, body: Box<Expr> @}
@end example

@item
Conditionals have a test, a consequent, and an alternative, all expressions.
The test must evaluate to a @code{Boolean}:

@example rust
Expr::If @{
    test: boxed(@r{…}),
    yes: boxed(@r{…}),
    no: boxed(@r{…}),
@}
@end example

@noindent
The completed checked language adds @code{if let} and the exhaustive
@code{match} with recursively admitted patterns.

@item
A block packages a sequence of statements into a single expression: the
selectors return the first statement and the rest of the statements in the
sequence, and the tail expression supplies the block's value.

@item
A procedure application is a call node.  The operator is one expression and
the operands are a vector:

@example rust
Expr::Call @{
    function: boxed(variable("factorial")),
    arguments: one(Expr::Subtract(
        boxed(variable("n")),
        boxed(integer(1)),
    )),
@}
@end example

@item
The primitive operations are constructor pairs: @code{Add}, @code{Subtract},
@code{Multiply}, and @code{Equal}.  The completed checked language admits the
unary and binary operators of the grammar: @code{i64} arithmetic
@code{+ - * / %}, @code{usize} addition and subtraction only, comparisons
between values of one integer type, and the short-circuit Boolean operators.
Integer overflow and division by zero trap; there is no implicit conversion.

@end itemize

@subsubheading Derived expressions

Some special forms in our language can be defined in terms of expressions
involving other special forms, rather than being implemented directly.  One
example is the case-analysis form of @ref{Exercise 4.5}, which can be
implemented as a nest of @code{If} expressions.  For example, we can reduce
the problem of evaluating a case over the value of @code{x} with the arms
@code{x > 0} yielding @code{x}, @code{x == 0} yielding a printed
@code{zero} and then @code{0}, and a final arm yielding @code{-x}, to the
problem of evaluating the following expression involving @code{If}
expressions and a block:

@example
If(x > 0,
   x,
   If(x == 0,
      Block[print("zero"), 0],
      Negate(x)))
@end example

@noindent
Implementing the evaluation of the case form in this way simplifies the
evaluator because it reduces the number of special forms for which the
evaluation process must be explicitly specified.  The predicate
@code{is_case} recognizes the form, the selector @code{case_arms} reads its
arms, and the function @code{case_to_if} transforms case expressions into
@code{If} expressions.  A case has a list of test-action arms, and an
@code{Else} arm is one whose test is the distinguished @code{Else} form.

@noindent
Expressions (such as the case form) that we choose to implement as syntactic
transformations are called @newterm{derived expressions}.  @code{Let}
expressions are also derived expressions (see @ref{Exercise 4.6}).@footnote{In
the old Lisp tradition, practical systems provided a mechanism that allowed a
user to add new derived expressions and specify their implementation as
syntactic transformations without modifying the evaluator.  Such a
user-defined transformation is called a @newterm{macro}.  Rust's macros are
outside the guest subset---the only admitted macros are @code{vec!} forms---so
in this edition the transformations live as functions over the typed syntax
data of the evaluator, and the exercises add forms there.  There has been much
research on mechanisms for macro definition that do not cause these
difficulties.  See, for example, @ref{Kohlbecker 1986},
@ref{Clinger and Rees 1991}, and @ref{Hanson 1991}.}

