@subsection Evaluator Data Structures

In addition to defining the external syntax of expressions, the evaluator
implementation must also define the data structures that the evaluator
manipulates internally, as part of the execution of a program, such as the
representation of procedures and environments and the representation of true
and false.

@subsubheading Testing of predicates

For conditionals, the object language demands an explicit Boolean; the
evaluator matches it directly.

@example rust
match evaluate(test, environment, global, program, store)? @{
    Value::Boolean(true) => @r{…},
    Value::Boolean(false) => @r{…},
    _ => Err(Fault::Type),
@}
@end example

@noindent
If an object language admitted truthiness, the interpreter predicate
@code{is_true} would translate an object value into the host's @code{bool};
that translation is exactly where a metacircular representation of truth can
differ from the host's.

@subsubheading Representing procedures

To handle procedures, the object language has two kinds of applicable value.
A top-level function is the index of its definition in the program's function
table; a closure carries its parameters, its body, and the environment it
captured:

@example rust
enum Value @{
    Integer(i64),
    Boolean(bool),
    Function(usize),
    Closure @{
        parameters: Vec<String>,
        body: Box<Expr>,
        environment: usize,
    @},
@}
@end example

@noindent
The closure constructor packages parameters and body with the current
environment; the evaluator builds it from a @code{Lambda} expression:

@example rust
Expr::Lambda @{ parameters, body @} => Ok(Value::Closure @{
    parameters: parameters.clone(),
    body: body.clone(),
    environment,
@}),
@end example

@noindent
A procedure's definition carries the parameter names and the body the
@code{FunctionDef} gives it, and @code{apply} installs the arguments in a
fresh frame.  It does not matter how we represent the applicable values, so
long as @code{apply} can identify and dispatch them.  In the completed
checked language the corresponding values are typed: top-level functions have
@code{FunId}s in the checked program, and admitted escaping closures are
boxed closure trait objects such as @code{Box<dyn Fn(i64) -> i64 + 'static>},
whose captures obey Rust's ownership rules.

@subsubheading Operations on Environments

The evaluator needs operations for manipulating environments.  As explained in
@ref{3.2}, an environment is a sequence of frames, where each frame is
a table of bindings that associate variables with their corresponding values.
We use the following operations for manipulating environments:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{lookup(store, frame, name)} returns the value that is bound to the name
in the environment, or signals @code{Fault::Unbound} if the variable is
unbound.

@item
@code{new_frame(store, parent)} returns a fresh frame whose enclosing
environment is the given parent; binding the parameters associates each
parameter with the value at the same position in the argument vector.

@item
Defining a variable adds to the first frame in the environment a binding that
associates the variable name with the value.

@item
Setting a variable to a new value in a specified environment searches for the
binding, just as in @code{lookup}, and changes the corresponding value when it
is found, or signals @code{Fault::Unbound}.

@end itemize

@noindent
To implement these operations we use the environments described in
@ref{3.2}; an environment is a chain of frames, and the enclosing environment
of an environment is the rest of the chain.  Each frame is a mutable table
from names to values, searched newest first, and the operations are thin
wrappers over that representation:

@example rust
struct Frame @{
    bindings: HashMap<String, Value>,
    parent: Option<usize>,
@}
struct Store @{ frames: Vec<Frame> @}

fn new_frame(store: &mut Store, parent: Option<usize>) -> usize @{
    let index = store.frames.len();
    store.frames.push(Frame @{ bindings: HashMap::new(), parent @});
    index
@}

fn lookup(
    store: &Store,
    mut frame: Option<usize>,
    name: &str,
) -> Option<Value> @{
    while let Some(index) = frame @{
        let current = &store.frames[index];
        if let Some(value) = current.bindings.get(name) @{
            return Some(value.clone());
        @}
        frame = current.parent;
    @}
    None
@}
@end example

@noindent
To extend an environment by a new frame that associates variables with values,
we make a frame binding each variable to the value at the same position, and
we @newterm{adjoin} this to the environment.  We signal an error if the number
of variables does not match the number of values.  To look up a variable in an
environment, we search the table of the first frame.  If we find the desired
variable, we return its value.  If we do not find the binding in the current
frame, we search the enclosing environment, and so on.  If no frame binds the
variable, we signal an ``unbound variable'' fault.  To set a variable to a new
value in a specified environment, we search for the variable, just as in
@code{lookup}, and change the corresponding value when we find it.  To define
a variable, we search the first frame for a binding of the variable and change
the binding if it exists (just as in setting); if no such binding exists, we
adjoin one to the first frame.

@noindent
The method described here is only one of many plausible ways to represent
environments.  Since we used data abstraction to isolate the rest of the
evaluator from the detailed choice of representation, we could change the
environment representation if we wanted to.  (See @ref{Exercise 4.11}.)  The
representation here, although conceptually simple, is not efficient and would
not ordinarily be used in a production system.@footnote{The drawback of this
representation (as well as the variant in @ref{Exercise 4.11}) is that the
evaluator may have to search through many frames to find the binding for a
given variable.  (Such an approach is referred to as a @newterm{deep
binding}.)  One way to avoid this inefficiency is to make use of a strategy
called @newterm{lexical addressing}, which will be discussed in @ref{5.5.6}.}

