@subsection Running the Evaluator as a Program

Given the evaluator, we have in our hands a description (expressed in Rust) of
the process by which the object language's expressions are evaluated.  One
advantage of expressing the evaluator as a program is that we can run the
program.  This gives us, running within Rust, a working model of how the
object language evaluates expressions.  This can serve as a framework for
experimenting with evaluation rules, as we shall do later in this chapter.

Our evaluator program is itself a guest program: it is ordinary Rust source
in the admitted subset.  The minimum such program is the evaluator witness of
the language contract, @code{spec/host-subsets/rust/programs/selfinterp.rs}.
It defines the typed data @code{Expr}, @code{Value}, @code{Frame},
@code{Store}, and @code{Fault}, and the functions @code{evaluate},
@code{apply}, and @code{execute_program} this section has been quoting.  The
program is admitted before any effect and run by the same engines that run any
other guest source:

@example rust
let program = sicp_runtime::host::admit(source)?;
let outcome = ch04::sec_4_1::run(&program);
assert_eq!(outcome.stdout, "120\n42\n42\n1\n2\n");
@end example

@noindent
The @code{main} of the witness constructs its two test programs and then
exercises the guest's own closures:

@example rust
fn main() @{
    match execute_program(&make_program()) @{
        Ok((Value::Integer(factorial), Value::Integer(captured))) => @{
            println!("@{@}", factorial);
            println!("@{@}", captured);
        @}
        _ => println!("evaluator error"),
    @}
    let add = make_adder(2);
    println!("@{@}", add(40));
    let mut counter = make_counter();
    println!("@{@}", counter());
    println!("@{@}", counter());
@}
@end example

@noindent
The first line is the evaluator computing @code{5!} from the constructed
factorial program; the second is the evaluator computing @code{3 + 39} with a
closure that captures @code{offset}; the third is the direct closure
@code{make_adder}; the last two are the escaping mutable closure
@code{make_counter}.  The complete transcript is five lines:

@example rust
let outcome = ch04::sec_4_1::run_source(source)?;
println!("@{@}", outcome.stdout);
// => 120
// => 42
// => 42
// => 1
// => 2
@end example

@noindent
The witness is the contract's minimum self-interpreter: its
@code{evaluate} and @code{apply} interpret typed @code{Expr} programs, and the
teaching engines admit and run the witness itself as guest source; the native
compiler runs the same source for comparison.  The full self-interpretation
test, a translated evaluator covering every admitted construct, is
@ref{Exercise 5.50}.  Nothing routes through a
host @code{eval} facility: @code{evaluate} is an ordinary guest function, and
the engines' entry points are @code{run}, @code{run_analyzed}, and, in
@ref{5.4} and @ref{5.5}, the explicit-control and compiled runners.  Calling
the host's production evaluator, reflection, or a host eval primitive would
not satisfy the self-interpretation requirement.

The interpreter's top-level bindings are installed the same way the evaluator
installs any environment:

@example rust
let global = new_frame(&mut store, None);
for (index, definition) in program.functions.iter().enumerate() @{
    store.frames[global].bindings.insert(
        definition.name.clone(),
        Value::Function(index),
    );
@}
@end example

@noindent
It does not matter how we represent the primitive procedure objects, so long
as @code{apply} can identify and apply them.  We have chosen to represent an
applicable top-level function as the index of its definition, and an
applicable closure as a value carrying its parameters, body, and captured
environment.  The guest's own first-class closures show the source-level
side of the same story:

@example rust
fn make_adder(addend: i64) -> Box<dyn Fn(i64) -> i64 + 'static> @{
    Box::new(move |value: i64| value + addend)
@}

fn make_counter() -> Box<dyn FnMut() -> i64 + 'static> @{
    let mut value = 0;
    Box::new(move || @{
        value += 1;
        value
    @})
@}
@end example

@noindent
For convenience in running the evaluator we provide a @newterm{driver} that
models the read-eval-print loop of an interactive system, in batch form: it
reads a complete program as source text, admits it, evaluates it, and prints
the result.  We precede each printed result by an @newterm{output prompt} in
the transcript convention of this edition so as to distinguish the value of
the expression from other output that may be printed:@footnote{The admission
step takes the source the user writes and returns the next complete checked
program.  For example, if the user writes @code{5 * 5} in a program, the
checker verifies it as a multiplication of two @code{i64} values.  There is no
quotation step: source is never data here; the object language's data are the
typed syntax values of @ref{4.1.2}.}

@example rust
let outcome = ch04::sec_4_1::run_source(source)?;
// => the stdout transcript, one @code{println!} result per line
@end example

@noindent
A raised fault ends the transcript with one error record: admission rejects
are typed @code{Diag} values raised before any effect, and execution faults
are typed @code{TrapReport} values raised with a source span.  We use the
checked printer for output values to avoid printing the environment part of a
compound procedure, which may be a very large arena index (or may even contain
cycles).

@noindent
Now all we need to do to run the evaluator is to create the global
environment and run programs in it.  Here is a sample interaction, with the
constructed program of the text:

@example rust
let outcome = ch04::sec_4_1::run_source(source)?;
// => 120
@end example

