@subsection Separating Syntactic Analysis from Execution

The evaluator implemented above is simple, but it is very inefficient, because
the syntactic analysis of expressions is interleaved with their execution.
Thus if a program is executed many times, its syntax is analyzed many times.
Consider, for example, evaluating @code{factorial(4)} using the following
definition of @code{factorial}:

@example rust
fn factorial(n: i64) -> i64 @{
    if n == 1 @{ 1 @} else @{ factorial(n - 1) * n @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Each time @code{factorial} is called, the evaluator must determine that the
body is an @code{If} expression and extract the predicate.  Only then can it
evaluate the predicate and dispatch on its value.  Each time it evaluates the
expression @code{factorial(n - 1) * n}, or the subexpressions
@code{factorial(n - 1)} and @code{n - 1}, the evaluator must perform the case
analysis in @code{evaluate} to determine that the expression is a call, and
must extract its operator and operands.  This analysis is expensive.
Performing it repeatedly is wasteful.

We can transform the evaluator to be significantly more efficient by arranging
things so that syntactic analysis is performed only once.@footnote{This
technique is an integral part of the compilation process, which we shall
discuss in @ref{Chapter 5}.  Jonathan Rees wrote an interpreter like this in
about 1982 for the T project (@ref{Rees and Adams 1982}).  Marc
@ref{Feeley (1986)} (see also @ref{Feeley and Lapalme 1987}) independently
invented this technique in his master's thesis.} We split @code{evaluate},
which takes an expression and an environment, into two parts.  The procedure
@code{analyze} takes only the expression.  It performs the syntactic analysis
and returns a new procedure, the @newterm{execution procedure}, that
encapsulates the work to be done in executing the analyzed expression.  The
execution procedure takes an environment as its argument and completes the
evaluation.  This saves work because @code{analyze} will be called only once on
an expression, while the execution procedure may be called many times.

In this edition the analyzer of @code{ch04::sec_4_1} makes the same split over
the checked source, and its execution procedures are data: a precomputed
@code{Plan} tree.  Building a plan resolves every dispatch decision once, and
running the plan never re-dispatches on syntax.  With the separation into
analysis and execution, evaluation becomes

@example rust
let program = admit(source)?;
// Analysis happens once per function; the run convenience
// analyzes the checked program and executes its plans.
let outcome = run_analyzed(&program);
@end example

@noindent
The result of calling @code{analyze} is one plan per function plus the entry
point:

@example rust
pub struct AnalyzedProgram @{
    pub sema: Sema,
    pub bodies: Vec<Plan>,
@}
@end example

@noindent
The @code{analyze} procedure performs the same case analysis as the original
@code{evaluate} of @ref{4.1.1}, except that the cases build only analysis,
not full evaluation.  A precomputed value is a plan that answers itself; a
place read is a precomputed root and projection sequence; a binding pairs a
fresh slot with its analyzed initializer:

@example rust
pub enum Plan @{
    Value(HostValue),
    Place @{ @r{…} @},
    @r{…}
@}

pub enum PlanStmt @{
    Let @{
        binding: BindId,
        destruct: Option<(BindId, BindId)>,
        value: Plan,
    @},
    @r{…}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Here is the simplest analysis, which handles self-evaluating expressions.  It
returns an execution procedure that ignores its environment and just returns
the value.  For a name, the binding is resolved to a slot at analysis time,
but the read of its value must still be done in the execution phase, since
this depends upon knowing the environment.@footnote{There is, however, an
important part of the variable search that @emph{can} be done as part of the
syntactic analysis.  As we will show in @ref{5.5.6}, one can determine
the position in the environment structure where the value of the variable will
be found, thus obviating the need to scan the environment for the entry that
matches the variable.}

@example rust
// The analyzed constant: a plan that answers itself.
Plan::Value(HostValue::Int(4))

// The analyzed place: root and projections resolved, read deferred.
PlanRoot::Local(binding)
@end example

@noindent
Assignment also must defer actually setting the variable until the execution,
when the environment has been supplied.  However, the fact that the
initializer expression can be analyzed (recursively) during analysis is a
major gain in efficiency, because the initializer will now be analyzed only
once.  The same holds true for bindings.

@noindent
For @code{If} expressions, we extract and analyze the predicate, consequent,
and alternative at analysis time; the plan's conditional node keeps the two
analyzed branches and selects one at execution.  Analyzing a closure also
achieves a major gain in efficiency: we analyze the closure body only once,
even though the closures resulting from evaluation may be applied many times.

@noindent
Analysis of a sequence of statements (as in a block or the body of a
procedure) is more involved.@footnote{See @ref{Exercise 4.23} for some insight
into the processing of sequences.} Each statement in the sequence is
analyzed, yielding an execution procedure.  These execution procedures are
combined to produce an execution procedure that takes an environment as
argument and sequentially runs each individual execution procedure with the
environment as argument.  At the top level the book's recursion combines two
procedures at a time; the analyzer records the whole sequence as one plan:

@example rust
fn analyze_block(block: &HirBlock) -> Plan @{
    @r{…}
@}
@end example

@noindent
To analyze a call, we analyze the operator and operands and construct an
execution procedure that runs the operator execution procedure (to obtain the
actual procedure to be applied) and the operand execution procedures (to
obtain the actual arguments), in left-to-right order.  We then pass these to
the application step, which is the analog of @code{apply} in
@ref{4.1.1}.  The application step differs from @code{apply} in that the
procedure body for a closure has already been analyzed, so there is no need to
do further analysis.  Instead, we just run the registered execution procedure
for the body on the extended environment.

@noindent
Our new evaluator uses the same data structures, syntax procedures, and
run-time support procedures as in @ref{4.1.2}, @ref{4.1.3}, and
@ref{4.1.4}.  Because the direct evaluator and the analyzer call the same
leaf semantics, values, effects, and traps match by construction, and the
cross-engine conformance checks hold every path to the same transcript.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.22}Exercise 4.22:} Extend the evaluator in this
section to support the derived binding form @code{Let}.  (See
@ref{Exercise 4.6}.)
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.23}Exercise 4.23:} Alyssa P. Hacker doesn't
understand why sequence analysis needs to be so complicated.  All the other
analysis procedures are straightforward transformations of the corresponding
evaluation procedures (or @code{evaluate} clauses) in
@ref{4.1.1}.  She expected sequence analysis to look like this, with the
sequence's procedures collected in a vector and run by a loop at execution
time:

@example rust
fn analyze_sequence(exps: &[Expr]) -> Result<Exec, Fault> @{
    let mut procs: Vec<Exec> = Vec::new();
    for exp in exps @{
        procs.push(analyze(exp)?);
    @}
    if procs.is_empty() @{ return Err(Fault::Type); @}
    Ok(move |env| @{
        let mut index = 0;
        while index + 1 < procs.len() @{
            procs[index](env)?;
            index += 1;
        @}
        procs[procs.len() - 1](env)
    @})
@}
@end example

Eva Lu Ator explains to Alyssa that the version in the text does more of the
work of evaluating a sequence at analysis time.  Alyssa's sequence-execution
procedure, rather than having the calls to the individual execution procedures
built in, loops through the procedures to call them: In effect, although the
individual expressions in the sequence have been analyzed, the sequence itself
has not been.

Compare the two versions of sequence analysis.  For example, consider the
common case (typical of procedure bodies) where the sequence has just one
expression.  What work will the execution procedure produced by Alyssa's
program do?  What about the execution procedure produced by the program in the
text above?  How do the two versions compare for a sequence with two
expressions?
@end quotation

